{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>A land which HaShem your Elohim seeks continually.</b> To visit its inhabitants and to scrutinize their deeds, for they are judged only according to the majority of their deeds. As it says: ״והיה אם שמוע תשמעו״ etc. ״אז ונתן מטר ארצכם בעתו״. And so it is concluded in the first chapter of Rosh HaShanah: ״תמיד עיני ה' אלהיך בה״ — sometimes for good, sometimes for bad. How so? If Israel were completely wicked on Rosh HaShanah and a small amount of rain was decreed for them, and in the end they repented — to add upon them [the rain]? One cannot, for the decree has already been issued. Rather, the Holy One, blessed be He, brings them down to a land that needs rain suited to times of bad. How so? etc., as is taught there." "<b>מרשית השנה.</b> Defective alef. From here Rabbi Yitzchak said in the first chapter of Rosh HaShanah: any year whose root is at its beginning is enriched at its end, as it is said: ״מראשית השנה ועד אחרית שנה״ — by inference, it has an end. That is, its root at its beginning [means] that the world is meritorious and conducts itself with lowliness and humility."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:00:51.954037449Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}